// A non-Copy value cannot silently satisfy a demand for OWNERSHIP.
//
// The other direction is legal, and deliberately: `own x` says the value is
// being given away and a plain binding takes ownership of what it is handed, so
// `let t: string = own s;` is the marker describing what the assignment already
// does. That direction is what makes `own o.field` — the way a field is taken
// out of a live value — usable at an ordinary binding, and it is pinned by the
// partial-move contract in sema.
//
// This direction stays refused: handing a plain non-Copy value where ownership
// is demanded would transfer it with nothing at the use site saying so.

fn main() {
    let s: string = "hi";
    let t: own string = s;
}
